<p class="sst-lead">Separability pays off here. Run a calibrated MMD test on the <strong>undulation</strong> channel
\(\bm{t}\) and, independently, on the <strong>scale</strong> channel \(\bm{\ell}\).</p>

<p>\[ \mathrm{pMMD}[\mathcal{H}\otimes\mathcal{Q},\hat{\bm{\rho}},\check{\bm{\rho}}] \;\Rightarrow\;
\text{FTR} \iff \big(H_0^{\bm{t}}\;\text{FTR}\big)\wedge\big(H_0^{\bm{\ell}}\;\text{FTR}\big). \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>The product fails to reject <em>only</em> when both channels do. Otherwise the rejecting channel <em>names the
  cause</em>: a difference in undulation (nonlinear) or in scale (linear). That's the "explainable" in EBC.</p>
  <p class="sst-note">Drive the two sliders: the highlighted truth-table cell and the verdict line track the
  four logical cases \((1\wedge1,\,1\wedge0,\,0\wedge1,\,0\wedge0)\).</p>
</div>
